> glib isn't the gnome library (officially it stands for "the G
> library", originally the G came because it was developed to help
> support GTK). It's a lower-level non-GUI library that has things like
> hash tables, data types with defined sizes, threads, networking event
> loops, objects, etc in a platform-independent way (so you can use the
> glib threads API and have a multithreaded app that works on Linux,
> Windows, MacOS, etc).  It also makes writing OO C code easier.  There
> are plenty of non-GUI, non-GTK, non-GNOME applications out there that
> use glib. Glib was developed by the gtk developers, but predates GNOME
> by a fair margin.
> 
> gtk is the "GIMP toolkit", a gui toolkit that was developed for the
> GIMP image manipulation program.  It uses glib for data structures,
> etc.    It's just a widget set (like Swing, Qt, Tk, etc) and many gtk
> applications aren't gnome applications at all (gtk was around well
> before the GNOME project ever started).
> 
> GNOME is a higher-level desktop environment that uses gtk, but it's
> just one user thereof.
> 
